CAT = "ea3Practice"; SEG = "PRA"
# Practice before the IRS: Circular 230 subparts A and B (31 CFR Part 10, Rev. 6-2014).
Q = [
("Under Circular 230, which activity is NOT practice before the IRS?",
 "Appearing as a witness", ["Filing a protest for a client", "Writing to an examiner", "Attending an Appeals conference"],
 "Section 10.8(b) lets any individual appear as a witness for the taxpayer or furnish information at the IRS's request. Preparing and filing documents, corresponding with the IRS and representing at conferences are practice under section 10.2."),
("Compared with an enrolled actuary, an enrolled agent may represent taxpayers on:",
 "Any matter before the IRS", ["Retirement plan issues only", "Returns the agent prepared", "Examinations, not collection"],
 "Section 10.3(c) gives an enrolled agent unlimited practice rights. An enrolled actuary is limited to listed retirement plan provisions, and an unenrolled preparer is limited to examinations of returns he prepared."),
("Without being a practitioner, who may represent a corporation before the IRS?",
 "A bona fide officer", ["Any shareholder", "Its outside bookkeeper", "A former director"],
 "Under section 10.7(c)(1)(iv), a bona fide officer or regular full-time employee may represent the corporation in limited practice. Owning shares or doing its books does not confer that right."),
("For Circular 230, an executor handling a decedent's tax matters is treated as:",
 "The taxpayer", ["A recognized representative", "A limited practitioner", "An unenrolled agent"],
 "Section 10.7(e) treats a fiduciary such as an executor, trustee or guardian as the taxpayer, not as a representative. The fiduciary may appoint a representative with Form 2848."),
("An examiner asks an EA for bank records that neither the EA nor the client holds. The EA must promptly:",
 "Say who may hold them", ["Get them from that person", "Verify the holder independently", "Wait for a summons first"],
 "Section 10.20(a)(2) requires the practitioner to notify the IRS and give any information he has about who may possess the records. He must ask the client but need not contact or verify others."),
("After telling a client about an error on a prior-year return, Circular 230 also requires the practitioner to:",
 "Explain its consequences", ["Report the error to the IRS", "File an amended return now", "Withdraw from the matter"],
 "Section 10.21 requires the practitioner to advise the client promptly of the error and of its consequences under the Code. It does not require disclosure to the IRS, an amendment or withdrawal."),
("A practitioner relies on an employee's work product. Under section 10.22 she is presumed diligent if she:",
 "Supervised the employee with care", ["Had the client approve the work", "Reviewed only the final totals", "Paid the employee a fair wage"],
 "Section 10.22(b) presumes due diligence when the practitioner used reasonable care in engaging, supervising, training and evaluating the person whose work she relied on."),
("An EA keeps requesting audit postponements only to run out the clock. This breaches the rule on:",
 "Prompt disposition of matters", ["Due diligence as to accuracy", "Conflicting client interests", "Return of a client's records"],
 "Section 10.23 says a practitioner may not unreasonably delay the prompt disposition of any matter before the IRS. Stalling an examination for its own sake is that kind of delay."),
("May an EA have a suspended CPA draft her responses to an IRS examiner?",
 "No, the work is practice", ["Yes, if she signs them", "Yes, with client consent", "Only for amended returns"],
 "Section 10.24 bars knowingly accepting assistance from a suspended or disbarred person when the assistance relates to practice before the IRS. Her signature or the client's consent does not cure it."),
("A former IRS employee personally and substantially worked on a client's audit. He may later represent that client in that matter:",
 "Never", ["After one year", "After two years", "With IRS consent"],
 "Section 10.25(b)(2) bars representing or knowingly assisting a party in a particular matter the former employee personally and substantially participated in. The two-year bar applies only to matters under his official responsibility."),
("A firm hires a former IRS agent barred from a client's matter. The firm may keep the client if it:",
 "Isolates the former agent", ["Tells the client in writing", "Waits until two years pass", "Gets the examiner's approval"],
 "Section 10.25(c) lets the firm represent the party only if it isolates the former employee from the matter, confirmed by a statement under oath kept by the firm."),
("May an EA who is a notary notarize a client's affidavit for an IRS matter she is handling?",
 "No, she is the agent in it", ["Yes, if commissioned", "Yes, with a Form 2848", "Only for her own clients"],
 "Section 10.26 bars a practitioner from acting as a notary on any IRS matter for which she is employed as counsel, attorney or agent, or in which she has any interest."),
("A contingent fee is allowed on an amended return filed within how many days of a written notice of examination?",
 "120", ["100", "90", "180"],
 "Section 10.27(b)(2)(ii) allows a contingent fee for an amended return or refund claim filed within 120 days of the taxpayer receiving written notice of examination of, or a challenge to, the original return."),
("A preparer charges 15% of the refund shown on a client's original 2025 return. Under Circular 230 the fee is:",
 "A prohibited contingent fee", ["Allowed if fully disclosed", "Allowed for refund returns", "Allowed with client consent"],
 "A fee based on a percentage of the refund is a contingent fee under section 10.27(c), and none of the exceptions covers preparing an original return."),
("Which contingent fee arrangement does Circular 230 allow?",
 "Penalty or interest refund claim", ["Preparing an original return", "Year-end tax planning advice", "Share of first-year tax savings"],
 "Section 10.27(b)(3) allows a contingent fee for a claim for credit or refund filed solely about statutory interest or penalties the IRS assessed. Fees tied to taxes saved are contingent and otherwise barred."),
("A preparer promises to refund his fee if the IRS disallows a deduction. Circular 230 treats this as:",
 "A contingent fee", ["A best practice", "An allowed guarantee", "An engagement term only"],
 "Section 10.27(c)(1) includes any arrangement to reimburse all or part of the fee if a position is challenged or not sustained, whether by indemnity, guarantee or rescission right."),
("Pending payment of his fee, which item may a practitioner hold back from a client?",
 "The return he prepared", ["The client's Forms W-2", "The client's bank statements", "A return another preparer did"],
 "Under section 10.28(b), documents the practitioner prepared and is withholding pending payment for that document are not client records. Documents the client or a third party supplied must be returned."),
("State law lets a practitioner keep records during a fee dispute. Under section 10.28 he must still return:",
 "Records to attach to the return", ["Every record the client gave him", "Nothing until the fee is paid", "Only the original receipts"],
 "When state law allows retention in a fee dispute, section 10.28(a) requires return only of records that must be attached to the taxpayer's return, plus reasonable access to review and copy the rest."),
("Clients consent to a conflict of interest orally. The written confirmation must follow no later than:",
 "30 days afterward", ["10 days afterward", "60 days afterward", "The end of the matter"],
 "Section 10.29(b)(3) allows the written confirmation within a reasonable period after the informed consent, but in no event later than 30 days."),
("A practitioner must keep clients' written conflict waivers for at least:",
 "36 months after the representation ends", ["3 years after they are signed", "12 months after the representation ends", "Until the next enrollment renewal"],
 "Section 10.29(c) requires keeping the written consents at least 36 months from the conclusion of the representation and giving them to the IRS on request."),
("Which description may an enrolled agent use in advertising?",
 "Enrolled to practice before the IRS", ["Certified by the IRS as a tax agent", "Certified enrolled agent of the IRS", "Tax agent employed by the IRS"],
 "Section 10.30(a)(1) bars an enrolled agent from using the term certified or implying an employment relationship with the IRS. Enrolled to practice before the IRS is listed as acceptable."),
("After publishing an hourly rate, a practitioner may not charge more than that rate for at least:",
 "30 calendar days", ["10 calendar days", "60 calendar days", "90 calendar days"],
 "Section 10.30(b)(2) caps fees at the published rates for at least 30 calendar days after the last date the fee schedule was published."),
("For a direct-mail advertising campaign, a practitioner must keep a copy of the mailing and:",
 "A list of who received it", ["Each recipient's written consent", "IRS approval of the wording", "The printer's invoice"],
 "Section 10.30(c) requires keeping a copy of the actual communication and a list or description of persons it went to, for at least 36 months from the last use."),
("Under section 10.30, a lawful written solicitation of a prospective client must:",
 "Say it is a solicitation", ["Be sent by certified mail", "Quote its fee schedule", "Carry the IRS e-file logo"],
 "Any lawful solicitation must clearly identify itself as a solicitation and, if applicable, the source of the information used to choose the recipient."),
("A client asks that her refund be deposited to the EA's account to cover his fee. The EA:",
 "Cannot accept the deposit", ["May accept with written consent", "May keep just his fee", "May accept if he files Form 8888"],
 "Section 10.31 bars directing or accepting a government payment for a client's federal tax liability into an account the practitioner or his firm owns or controls."),
("An EA advised a client on a return position. Under section 10.34(c) she must explain likely penalties:",
 "Even if she faces no penalty", ["Only if the client asks", "Only if she could be penalized", "Only after an audit begins"],
 "Section 10.34(c) requires informing the client of penalties reasonably likely to apply and of any chance to avoid them by disclosure, even when the practitioner faces no penalty herself."),
("A practitioner may advise a client to take a position on an affidavit filed with the IRS only if the position is:",
 "Not frivolous", ["More likely than not", "Backed by substantial authority", "Disclosed on Form 8275"],
 "Section 10.34(b)(1) sets the standard for documents, affidavits and other papers: the position must not be frivolous. Return positions carry the higher reasonable basis and section 6694 standards."),
("An EA takes on a trust matter she has never handled. Circular 230 lets her become competent by:",
 "Studying the relevant law", ["Telling the client she is new", "Charging a lower fee", "Getting a signed waiver"],
 "Section 10.35 says a practitioner may become competent for the matter through methods such as consulting experts in the area or studying the relevant law."),
("A firm names no one responsible for its Circular 230 compliance procedures. Then:",
 "The IRS may name someone", ["The managing partner is by law", "Each partner shares the duty", "The largest owner is named"],
 "Section 10.36(a) says that if a firm identifies no one with principal authority for compliance procedures, the IRS may identify one or more individuals responsible."),
("In giving written tax advice, which factor may a practitioner NOT take into account?",
 "Odds of no audit", ["Assumed future events", "Facts the client gave", "Relevant case law"],
 "Section 10.37(a)(2)(vi) bars weighing the possibility that a return will not be audited or an issue will not be raised. Reasonable assumptions, including about future events, are allowed."),
("Over a full three-year enrollment cycle, an enrolled agent needs how many CE hours?",
 "72, including 6 of ethics", ["48, including 6 of ethics", "72, including 3 of ethics", "60, including 2 of ethics"],
 "Section 10.6(e)(2)(i) requires at least 72 hours of continuing education per enrollment cycle, including six hours of ethics or professional conduct."),
("In each enrollment year, an enrolled agent must complete at least:",
 "16 hours, 2 of them ethics", ["24 hours, 2 of them ethics", "16 hours, 4 of them ethics", "12 hours, 1 of them ethics"],
 "Section 10.6(e)(2)(ii) sets a yearly minimum of 16 continuing education hours, including two hours of ethics or professional conduct."),
("A CE webinar runs 90 minutes without a break. How many CE hours does it earn?",
 "1", ["1.5", "2", "0.5"],
 "A contact hour is 50 minutes, and section 10.6(g)(2) grants credit only for full contact hours. A program over 50 but under 100 minutes counts as one hour."),
("An EA first enrolled partway through an enrollment cycle must complete CE of:",
 "2 hours per month enrolled", ["16 hours for each year left", "Nothing until the next cycle", "72 hours before renewal"],
 "Under section 10.6(e)(2)(iii), an individual enrolled during a cycle needs two hours for each month enrolled, counting a part month as a full month, plus two ethics hours per enrollment year."),
("An enrolled agent who moves must notify the IRS of the new address within:",
 "60 days", ["10 days", "30 days", "90 days"],
 "Section 10.6(c) requires notice of a change of address within 60 days. That notice does not change the practitioner's last known address for deficiency notices."),
("An EA who represents clients but prepares no returns must hold:",
 "A current PTIN", ["No PTIN at all", "An EFIN instead", "Only a CAF number"],
 "Section 10.6(d)(2)(i) requires every enrolled agent to renew a preparer tax identification number as the IRS prescribes, whether or not the agent prepares returns."),
]
